  body: "# Incapacity Benefit: the captured legacy claim boundary\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe Incapacity
    Benefit introduction records its replacement of earlier sickness/invalidity benefits and a closure to claims
    from 31 January 2011, subject to the exception it signposts for certain existing disability-related Income Support
    claimants.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nThis is legacy guidance, not a new claim route for ESA or Universal
    Credit. The source retains contribution, incapacity and transitional provisions. The exception, chapter 45 and
    statutory versions need review before applying the date to any real claim.\n\n## Inspected passages\n\n- [DMG
    56001, PDF page 5](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a80bf45ed915d74e623036c/dmgch56.pdf#page=5).\n\
    - [DMG 56002, PDF page 5](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a80bf45ed915d74e623036c/dmgch56.pdf#page=5).\n\
    - [DMG 56004, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a80bf45ed915d74e623036c/dmgch56.pdf#page=7).\n\
    \n## Evidence and review status\n\nIndependent experimental interpretation of captured DWP guidance, not an
    official DWP document. Definitions and semantic associations are model-assisted proposals awaiting specialist
    review. No individual entitlement decision, medical conclusion, award calculation or executable rule is provided.\n\
    \nSource observation, listing-page update, printed revision, legal event and claimant-event dates are separate.
    Current legal applicability is unresolved. Page numbers are one-based PDF positions; machine extraction may
    damage reading order, characters and footnotes. No visual PDF or specialist acceptance is claimed.\n\n- `dmg-vol10-ch56`:
    PDF `24fde3944e7e2605c24baf711da1ace4dfe0032fe13b6bfc169e4562bf5ecca5`; extracted pages `b5dc3260133af75807e6248202525da77a6610befc0b7ff20bc883d2ebba530e`;
    acquired `2026-09-15T19:45:32.379452Z`.\n"
  resource: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5a80bf45ed915d74e623036c/dmgch56.pdf#page=5
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/ib-youth-overlap-classification
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source expressly calls IB(Y) a category of IB, not a separate benefit.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol10-ch56/0005
      quote: "56002   Entitlement to IB depends, at the outset, on satisfying certain contribution\n        conditions.
        IB is payable at three basic rates according to the length of time the\n        incapacity has lasted. A
        person who does not satisfy the contribution conditions may\n        still qualify for IB(Y). This is a
        category of IB and is not a separate benefit (see DMG"
      locator: DMG 56002
  - target: term/ib-contribution-record-referral
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: Contribution conditions and a disputed contribution record are separate source questions.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol10-ch56/0007
      quote: "56008   Entitlement to IB depends on the contribution conditions being satisfied. In practice\n  \
        \      the NI contribution record is usually obtained and any decision is based on the\n        assumption
        that the record is factually correct. However, where there is a dispute\n        about the record, the matter
        must be referred by the DM to HMRC for a formal\n        decision1.\n\n        Note 1: See DMG Chapters
        03, 04 and 06 for guidance on how decisions and\n        appeals are handled after a reference to HMRC.\n\
        \n\n\n\nVol 10 Amendment 33 February 2012"
      locator: DMG 56008
